item.text:
{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q003_actin_like_atpase_divergence","answer":"SCOPe 2.08 places these catalytic domains in distinct families within the same Actin-like ATPase domain superfamily, c.55.1 (SUNID 53067): Actin/HSP70 is c.55.1.1, hexokinase is c.55.1.3, and glycerol kinase is c.55.1.4. This superfamily belongs to the Ribonuclease H-like motif fold, c.55, within the alpha/beta protein class, c.

Their shared superfamily supports remote homology: descent from a common ancestral domain despite substantial sequence and functional divergence. It supports interpreting their related ATP-binding and catalytic architecture as an evolutionary inheritance. Sharing only a fold would establish structural similarity without necessarily establishing common ancestry; sharing a class is broader still. The separate families distinguish more closely related groups within this ancient superfamily.

This relationship does not make their functions interchangeable. Actin couples nucleotide turnover to cytoskeletal dynamics, HSP70 uses ATP cycling in chaperone activity, hexokinase phosphorylates hexoses, and glycerol kinase phosphorylates glycerol. Neither the superfamily name nor family membership alone licenses transferring substrate specificity, reaction details, cellular role, or interaction partners. Even actin and HSP70 share a SCOPe family while performing different roles. The relationship concerns the classified domains, not necessarily every domain of the complete proteins. Cross-family structures can guide comparison of the conserved core, but reliable detailed modeling or functional assignment requires sequence/structure alignment, conservation of relevant active-site residues, and biochemical evidence; one member is not automatically an adequate representative of all these functions.","evidence_used":["Pretrained protein structure and function knowledge","mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=actin&mode=auto&limit=20","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=glycerol+kinase&mode=auto&limit=20","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1/children?limit=100"]}]}
